<ol start="2" data-start="765" data-end="853">
<li data-start="765" data-end="853">
<p data-start="768" data-end="853">Deploy with <strong data-start="780" data-end="810"><code data-start="782" data-end="808">next build &amp;&amp; next start</code></strong> (a real Next.js app), not <code data-start="837" data-end="850">next export</code>.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="855" data-end="864">That way:</p>
<ul data-start="865" data-end="962">
<li data-start="865" data-end="895">
<p data-start="867" data-end="895">Old products are prebuilt.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="896" data-end="962">
<p data-start="898" data-end="962">New products are generated <strong data-start="925" data-end="945">on first request</strong> → no more 404.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="964" data-end="967">
<h3 data-start="969" data-end="985">⚠️ Important</h3>
<p data-start="986" data-end="1198">Static hosting on <strong data-start="1004" data-end="1019">Creta Force</strong> only supports <code data-start="1034" data-end="1047">next export</code>.<br data-start="1048" data-end="1051">
So if you want <code data-start="1066" data-end="1088">fallback: 'blocking'</code> to actually work → you’ll need to host on <strong data-start="1131" data-end="1152">Azure App Service</strong> (or Vercel, Render, etc.), not a static host.</p>
<hr data-start="1200" data-end="1203">
